specs
# Claude Code kit — LOCAL, never versioned. A tool for whoever develops this, not product
# code: someone who clones TheoCode gets the agent, not the maintenance scaffolding of the
# people who write it. Install it with `bash <squad>/scripts/install.sh .` when wanted.
.claude/

# Secrets — never commit (.env.example IS committed as a template)
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# theokit runtime state (memory sessions, agent registry) — local, not source
.theokit/*
# M24 — project skills are source (committed); the rest of .theokit/ is runtime state
!.theokit/skills/
!.theokit/agents/
.theokit/agents/registry.json*

# Rules inherited from the previous kit, back when `.claude/` WAS versioned. They stay as
# defensive redundancy — all of `.claude/` is already ignored above, and a rule that can no
# longer match anything is noise, not protection. If someone reverts that decision, these apply again.
.claude/**/__pycache__/
.claude/**/*.pyc
.claude/knowledge-base/references/**
.claude/knowledge-base/tools/**
.claude/knowledge-base/discoveries/snapshots/**
.claude/agents/**
bench/reports/run.log
architecture-output/

# MCP servers config (user/local, like Codex config) — copy .mcp.json.example to enable
.mcp.json
# M110 (review) — the backup pattern too. `.mcp.json` was covered and `.mcp.json.bak-*` was not,
# so a `git add -A` swept two of them into the repo. There was no credential in them (verified:
# zero hits for authorization/bearer/token/secret/key/password), but the name `.bak-bearer` shows
# the intent was to snapshot the variant carrying an authorization header — the next copy could.
.mcp.json.bak*

# Compaction snapshots (audit trail, local)
.claude/.compaction-snapshots/

# harness-comparison live fixtures (local)
.heval/

# M20 — per-user/project runtime config (like .theokit/, not committed)
# B-017 — `.theocode/` followed by `!.theocode/` cancelled itself: git re-included the directory and
# everything under it, so runtime state (sessions, pointers, the resolved config) was committable
# despite the comment above declaring it local. Ignoring the CONTENTS keeps the exception working —
# a negation cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, which is why the previous
# spelling needed the directory itself listed.
.theocode/*
!.theocode/config.example.toml

# Scratch probes from review agents — working files, never deliverables.
# M98 (ADV-7 audit) — the pattern was a LITERAL `.probe/`, and the M98 review session created
# `.m98probe/`, which it did not cover: the tree went dirty and both `cycle-review.md §
# Pre-conditions` ("no uncommitted changes") and `cycle-code-quality.md § Anti-patterns` (never
# audit a dirty tree) started being violated by a file the rule already meant to exclude.
.*probe/
.*fix/
cross-validation-output/

# loop-deadcode-audit plugin output. Same rule as `code-review-output` above: the durable
# deliverable is the CHANGELOG entry and the `knip.jsonc` gate this run produced, not the
# SQLite database and the intermediate finding files.
deadcode-output/

# M58 error-handling-gate test temp dirs (created/removed per test)
.eh-gate-*/
.m67-fixtures-*/
.m104-fixture-*/
.m104-sentinel-*/
# Halt-loop runtime checkpoint — `cycle-implement.md:128` declares it gitignored.
.claude/knowledge-base/**/.progress-*.json
# State file of the loop-system-design plugin — a session draft, never versioned.
.claude/system-design-loop.local.md
# Phase 7 gate fixture — the third of the three prefixes; it leaks when the worker dies.
.m104-f7-*/

# loop-system-design plugin output (the durable report lives in knowledge-base/audits/)
system-design-output/
.m71-audit/
.pr-body.md

# Artifacts of the quality tools and the review cycle (M1, #322).
# `.review-runs/` holds the per-pillar JSON findings of ONE `/review` run; what lasts is the
# consolidated report in `.claude/knowledge-base/reviews/`. `.pytest_cache/` and `.ruff_cache/`
# are tool caches and appear every time the gate runs.
.review-runs/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Per-invocation halt-loop drivers (`/implement`, `/plan-improve`). They are generated from
# `.claude/skills/*/prompts/*.md` with the placeholders substituted, and rebuilt on every
# invocation — versioning them would produce a derived-file diff on every run.
halt-loop-prompts/

# kit installer artifact — dated copies on every propagation
.claude/.patch-backups/

# PAIRED skills generated by `/review` (one per reviewer, per invocation). They are gate
# scaffolding, not project knowledge: `spawn_reviewers.py` recreates them on every run, and
# versioning them would fill `skills/` with dead `review-{slug}-{role}-knowledge/` directories.
# The audit trail that MATTERS — the agent definitions and the findings — lives in
# `.claude/agents/review-*/`, and that one is versioned.
.claude/skills/review-*-knowledge/

# The OpenAI Codex study clone. NEVER commit it — this is a LICENCE boundary, not tidiness.
#
# `README.md` states the rule this entry enforces: "The study clone lived outside the tree,
# gitignored, read-only … a literal copy would carry the upstream licence into this repository,
# which is a legal problem and not a stylistic one." `NOTICE` records what was derived from it and
# how; every derivation is design re-expressed in our own code, and none of it is Codex source.
#
# Measured 2026-08-25: `codex/` was present, 99 MB, untracked AND unignored — so it was not
# outside the tree and not gitignored, and `git add -A` would have carried Apache-2.0 source into
# a repository that is public (`gh api repos/usetheoai-lab/TheoCode --jq '{private}'` -> false).
# Nothing had flagged it: `git status` shows it as one untracked directory line like any other.
#
# It is a legitimate thing to have on disk — it is the reference the parity work is measured
# against. What must not happen is it entering a commit.
codex/

# One package manager, one lockfile — see pnpm-workspace.yaml.
package-lock.json

# Session transcripts dumped by the TUI export; evidence for one run, not project docs.
/tui-*-*-*-*-*.md

# A scratch agent definition that appeared in the working tree and was swept into a
# release commit by `git add -A`. It is environment-specific (it names a local MCP server)
# and is nobody's source.
deep_researcher.yaml
